h  I open [the] door; I examine my shoes, which [my] valet (}{\i\f22 boy}{\f19 ) did not want to polish.\par 8.\emdash  At length the maid-servant returns; I have (}{\i\f22 take}{\f19 ) [my] breakfast straightaway. \par 9.\emdash  Ouch (}{\i\f22 woe}{\f19 )! My throat is burning (}{\i\f22 throat is burned to me}{\f19 )! Now [the/my] coffee is boiling!\par 10.\emdash  All this (}{\i\f22 all these things}{\f19 ) has made me late (}{\i\f22 made delay to-me}{\f19 )! To catch the train on its way (}{\i\f22 towards the-train that-is-to-be-caught-up-with}{\f19 ), I have to run (}{\i\f22 it-behooves that-I-run}{\f19 ).\par }{\b\f21 \par Proverbs}{\f19 \par 11.\emdash  We must do our selves what (}{\i\f22 that which}{\f19 ) we want to see to more properly (}{\i\f22 more-rightly}{\f19 )!\par 12.\emdash  We have as many enemies as we have servants (}{\i\f22 how many servants, so many enemies}{\f19 ).\par \par NOTES\par }{\b\f21 1. S}{\f19 u}{\b\f21 mere}{\f19  (}{\b\f21 s}{\f19 u}{\b\f21 mo}{\f19 , }{\b\f21 s}{\f19 u}{\b\f21 mis}{\f19 , etc.), }{\i\f22 to take, seize, assume, swallow}{\f19  (to eat or drink). Its derivative }{\b\f21 cons}{\f19 u}{\b\f21 mere}{\f19 , }{\i\f22 to consume, destroy}{\f19  gave the French language both the verb }{\i\f22 consumer}{\f19  (}{\i\f22 to consume, destroy}{\f19 ) and its popular doublet }{\i\f22 consommer}{\f19  (}{\i\f22 to consume, use up}{\f19 ). A thousand and one vicissitudes of this sort came to Latin words in the course of their passage into the vernaculars.\par }{\b\f21 2. C}{\f19 o}{\b\f21 rpus}{\f19  is a neuter noun. Both in the singular and in the plural the neuter nouns have the same form when they are subjects and when they are direct objects; plural }{\b\f21 c}{\f19 o}{\b\f21 rpora}{\f19 . In our sentence, there is no need to say }{\b\f21 l}{\f19 a}{\b\f21 vo c}{\f19 o}{\b\f21 rpus m}{\f19 e}{\b\f21 um}{\f19 , because it is obvious that it is my own body which I wash and not that of others!\par }{\b\f21 3. P